WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.960
I’m often asked
what my opinion

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.520
about a diet
or disease is.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.840
Who cares what my or
anyone else’s opinion is.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.920
All we should care about
is what the science says.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.080
What does the best available
balance of evidence

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.000
Published in the peer-
reviewed medical literature

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.280
have to say right now?

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.720
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.280
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.520
Today we examine the
sketchy supplement industry.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.840
And by sketchy,
I don’t mean artistic.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.720
In our first story,
we take a close look

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.000
at the industry’s attempts
to rebut an editorial

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.840
in an alternative
medicine journal.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.680
An editorial was recently published decrying
much of the multibillion dollar marketing

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.480
of dietary supplements in North America as
misleading, deceptive — even predatory.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.360
After examining hundreds of sales claims
made when supplements are being marketed

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.840
over the years, the author concluded:
"Dishonesty or wild exaggerations are

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.880
frequent occurrences in the
marketing of supplements."

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.840
My favorite quote was: "The marketers
of supplements like to use scientific

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.360
evidence the way a drunk uses a lamp-post:
more for support than illumination."

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.000
This is nothing new.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.728
Similar editorials have been published
in the Journal of the American Medical

00:01:22.728 --> 00:01:24.880
Association and the New England
Journal of Medicine.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.880
What made this special was that it was,
to their credit, published in

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.000
the Journal of Alternative and
Complementary Medicine.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.840
It’s like their own journal admitting it!

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.760
In response, the Head of Global Supplement
Initiatives at Novus, which actually

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.400
started out as the livestock feed
additives division of Monsanto, before

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.120
branching off into human feed additives —
dietary supplements — starts his rebuttal

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.480
with this counterpoint: "In his review
entitled ’The Marketing of Dietary

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.520
Supplements in North America: The Emperor
is (Almost) Naked,’ [the author] may be

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.680
mortified to know the emperor is still
dancing in the street, dressed or not,

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.320
to the tune of $68 billion.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.280
This figure is much higher and is a more
relevant number than cited… indicating

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.920
a very strong and respectable commerce."

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.400
That’s his first argument?

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.480
That it’s highly lucrative?

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.680
He goes on to say that hey, they’re
safer than some pharmaceuticals.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.600
But how much is that saying, given that
prescription drugs kill an estimated

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.880
106,000 Americans every year?

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.520
And that’s not errors;
not abuse; not overdose.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.560
That’s just deaths from side effects,
ADRs (adverse drug reactions), which

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.520
would make doctors — me and my fellow
colleagues — the fourth leading cause

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.360
of death in the United States.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.440
So, for the supplement industry to say,
"Hey, at least we’re not the fourth

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.080
leading cause of death,"
isn’t saying much.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.080
Next up, calcium supplements.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.481
Might they increase the
risk of heart attacks?

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:02.000
Here’s the story.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.720
In 12 short years, government panels
have gone from suggesting widespread

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.400
calcium supplementation may be
necessary to protect our bones to

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.760
"Do Not Supplement."
What happened?

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.000
It all started with a 2008
study in New Zealand.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.440
Short-term studies had showed that
calcium supplementation may drop

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.280
blood pressures by about a point,
though the effect appears transient,

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.560
disappearing after a few months,
but it's better than nothing.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.520
And excess calcium in the gut
can cause fat malabsorption,

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.680
by forming soap fat, reducing
saturated fat absorption

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.120
and increasing fecal
saturated fat content.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:46.720
And indeed if you take eat a couple Tums
along with your half bucket of KFC,

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.360
up to twice as much fat
could end up in your stool.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.360
And with less saturated fat absorbed in
your system your cholesterol might drop.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.640
So the New Zealand researchers were
expecting to lower heart attack rates

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:00.640
by giving women calcium supplements.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.080
To their surprise there appeared
to be more heart attacks

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.160
in the calcium supplement
group. Was this just a fluke?

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.280
All eyes turned to the Women's
Health Initiative, the largest and

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.960
longest randomized controlled trial
of calcium supplementation.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.920
The name might sound familiar.
That's the study that uncovered

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.720
how dangerous hormone
replacement therapy was.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.840
Would it do the same for
calcium supplements?

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.160
The Women's Health Initiative
reported no adverse effects.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:34.640
However, the majority
of the participants

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.320
were already taking calcium
supplements before the study started,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.280
so effectively the study was
just comparing higher versus

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.360
lower dose calcium supplementation,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.320
not calcium supplements versus
no calcium supplements.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.280
But what if you go back and just
see what happened to the women

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.200
who started out not taking supplements
and then were randomized

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:54.080
to the supplement group?

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.040
Those who started calcium
supplements suffered significantly

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.640
more heart attacks or strokes.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.360
Thus, high dose or low dose, any
calcium supplementation seems

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.000
to increase cardiovascular
disease risk.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.600
So researchers went back digging
through other trial data for heart attack

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.320
and stroke rates in women
randomized to calcium supplements,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.000
with or without vitamin D added,
and confirmed the danger.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.520
And most of the population
studies agreed:

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.160
users of calcium supplements
tended to have increased rates

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.640
of heart disease, stroke, and death.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.720
The supplement industry
was not happy,

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.160
accusing the researchers
of relying in part

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.080
on self-reported data -- like they just
ask if people had a heart attack or not

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.440
rather than verifying it.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.560
And indeed long-term
calcium supplementation

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.920
causes all sorts of
gastrointestinal distress

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.840
including twice the risk of being
hospitalized with acute symptoms

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.240
that may have been confused
with a heart attack.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.280
But no, the increased risk
was seen consistently

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.320
across the trials whether the
heart attacks were verified or not.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.440
OK, but why do calcium supplements
increase heart attack risk,

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.840
but not calcium you get in your diet?

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.960
Perhaps because when
you take calcium pills,

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.040
you get a spike of calcium
in your bloodstream

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.920
that you don't get just
eating calcium rich foods.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:21.920
Within hours of taking supplemental
calcium, the calcium levels

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.520
in the blood shoot up and can
stay up as long as eight hours.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:31.200
This evidently produces what's called a
hypercoaguable state -- your blood clots

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.760
more easily -- which could increase
the risk of clots in the heart and brain.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.880
And indeed higher
calcium blood levels

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.360
are tied to higher heart
attack and stroke rates.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.560
So the mechanism may
be calcium supplements

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.040
leading to unnaturally large, rapid, and
sustained calcium levels in the blood,

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:52.400
which can have a variety
of potentially problematic effects.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.360
Calcium supplements have been
widely embraced on the grounds that

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.800
they are a natural and therefore a safe
way of preventing osteoporotic fractures.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.240
But it's now becoming clear that taking
calcium in one or two daily doses

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.480
is not natural, in that it does not
reproduce the same metabolic effects

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.840
as calcium in food --
the way nature intended.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:17.360
And furthermore the evidence is
also becoming steadily stronger

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.160
that calcium supplementation
may not be safe.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.040
That's why most organizations
providing advice regarding bone health

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.160
now recommend that individuals
should obtain their calcium requirement

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.322
from diet in preference to supplements.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.353
Finally today, black raspberry
supplements are put to the test.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:42.240
The regulation of dietary supplements
in the United States

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:45.040
has been described as
too little, too late.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.600
Dietary supplements
may be adulterated

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.680
with dangerous compounds,
be contaminated,

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.080
fail to contain what
they say they contain,

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.160
contain unknown doses
of the ingredients

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:54.960
listed on
the label,

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.280
be sold at
toxic dosages,

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.600
or produce harmful
effects in other ways.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.800
This not only messes up any
research done on them,

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.120
but can put the
general public at risk.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:07.840
A third party company
that has tested

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:08.880
thousands of supplements,

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.760
identified approximately
1 in 4 with a quality problem,

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:13.440
either not containing
what it says

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.320
or contaminated
in some way – 1 in 4.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.720
For example, I’ve
done a few videos

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.600
on the remarkable properties
of black raspberries.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.440
Can’t always find
them fresh or frozen;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:25.920
so how about black
raspberry supplements?

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.160
You go to the store
or look online.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.520
How about this one?

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:32.640
Fresh, raw, pure—
that sounds good.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:33.600
Let’s look at the back.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.000
It says it contains just seedless
black raspberry powder

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.080
and absolutely nothing else –
exclamation point!

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:44.720
It’s nice to see
there are no fillers

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.320
or artificial
ingredients.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.320
So, you plunk
down your $23.77,

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.840
but it turns out
you’ve been had.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:56.720
The first clue was
that the picture

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.920
on the front was actually
blackberries photoshopped

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.680
to look like black
raspberries—

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.000
they couldn’t even be bothered
to put a real image

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.440
on their fake supplement.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:08.960
The researchers' second clue
was that it sure didn’t look

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.360
like pure black
raspberry powder;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.600
and so, they put
it to the test,

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.880
and indeed, there was no
black raspberry at all.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.400
Instead of absolutely
nothing else,

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.080
they should have just
stopped with

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.120
"This bottle contains
absolutely nothing!".

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.680
Or, at least you hope
it contains nothing—

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.480
who knows what’s actually
in those capsules.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.880
They tested every black raspberry
product they could find,

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:37.120
and even ones with the
right picture on the front

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:38.560
and powder that
actually looked real,

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:41.440
yet more than a third
appeared to have

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.920
no black raspberry
fruit at all.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.800
At the moment,
a consumer who assumes

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.680
the US dietary supplement
marketplace is free from risk

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.040
or even honest is
unfortunately naive.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.080
How widespread is
this deception?

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:59.360
Researchers used DNA
fingerprinting techniques

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:02.240
to test the authenticity
of 44 herbal supplements

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.080
from a dozen different
companies.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.480
Less than half of the
supplements were authentic,

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:09.760
containing what they
said they did.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:13.200
Most contained plants
not listed on the label,

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:16.720
substitutions with
cheaper plants,

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:20.160
contaminants, unlisted fillers,
or apparently all filler.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.880
And this isn’t
just fraud;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.360
some of this deception
can really hurt people.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:28.160
For example, one St. John’s
wort supplement

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.080
had no St. John’s
wort at all,

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:32.080
but actually contained
Senna instead,

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:33.760
which is an
herbal laxative

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:34.960
that can cause
adverse effects,

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:38.795
such as chronic diarrhea, liver damage,
skin breakdown, and blistering.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:42.240
Until dietary supplements in
the U.S. are better regulated

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.040
and quality control standards
are defined and endorsed,

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:47.440
the safer source of
phytonutrients

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:51.912
as a consumer is
from actual food.

